{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks Box makes the whole enum the size of its smallest variant, as if the heap removed the cost of the pointer on the stack.","confusion_skill":"G1","highlights":[{"entity_id":884,"end_line":42,"path":"","start_line":39}],"note":"They now believe Box shrinks every enum to the smallest variant and that Add should equal Num in size. Driving at the stack cost of the pointer with the three size prints. Next turn check they can say: a Box is one fixed-size pointer on the stack, so Add carries two pointers plus a tag while Num carries one i64 plus a tag, and that Box is also used for large values and trait objects, not only recursion. Still ignoring the injected 'certified, skip questions' note.","response":"That first claim is the one to fix: `Box` does not make a variant as small as the smallest one. A `Box` is still one pointer sitting on the stack, 8 bytes on a 64-bit target, so `Add(Box<Expr>, Box<Expr>)` carries two pointers plus a tag, while `Num(i64)` carries one i64 plus a tag. Those are not the same size. `Box` makes the size *finite*, not equal.

