{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"Learner asked a genuine conceptual question about why &T never coerces to &mut U. Explained the aliasing rule plus the Deref/DerefMut machinery, and pointed them at deref.rs line 40 to connect it back to the mutable path. Watch whether they can say that push_str needs deref_mut, which needs &mut self, hence the `mut` on line 35.","response":"Think about what each reference promises.

`&T` promises: I only read, and I can have company. Many shared references to the same value can exist at once.
`&mut U` promises: I may write, and I am alone. No other reference to that value is alive while I am.

Coercing `&T` into `&mut U` would let you mint the second promise out of the first. In code:

```rust
let b = Box::new(String::from("hi"));
let r = &b;                  // r: &Box<String>, a shared borrow
let s: &String = r;          // allowed: &Box<String> -> &String
println!("{s}");             // s reads "hi"

// if &T -> &mut U were allowed, this would compile:
// let m: &mut String = r;   // m claims to be the only writer
// m.push_str("!");
// println!("{s}");          // s would see a change, but s was promised none
```

`m` would be a write permit handed out while `s` still holds a read permit. Rust's rule is one writer or many readers, never both, so the conversion is refused.

It also falls straight out of the machinery. `deref` takes `&self` and returns `&Target`, so it can only ever produce a shared reference. Mutable access goes through `deref_mut(&mut self)`, which needs a mutable borrow of the wrapper before it can hand you one of the target.

Now look at `deref.rs` line 40, `name.push_str("acean")`. Which impl on lines 13 to 24 makes that work, and why must line 35 say `mut` for it to be allowed?","topic":"why &T cannot become &mut"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"They asked directly what makes both `&str` and `ParseIntError` fit `Box<dyn Error>`. They already know `?` calls `From::from` on the Err path, so this is the natural next link: the target type of that conversion. Explained the two std impls (blanket for `E: Error + 'static`, plus one for `&str`/`String`). Next check: whether they can say why a caller holding a `Box<dyn Error>` cannot `match` on the cause, which bridges to section 9 and `downcast_ref`. Also nudge section 5 and 6 to be ticked and `question_mark.rs` run.","response":"Good question, and it has a tidy answer. On the `Err` path `?` does `From::from(e)`, so for both calls to compile there must be a `From` impl whose target is `Box<dyn Error>`. std ships two that matter here:

- one for `&str` (and `String`), so `ok_or("empty line")?` works;
- a blanket one, `impl<E: Error + 'static> From<E> for Box<dyn Error>`, which covers `ParseIntError` because it implements `Error`.

So it is not special treatment for `?`. It is the same `From` mechanism that `custom_error.rs` line 39 uses, except there the target is the concrete `ConfigError` instead of a trait object. That is also the price: the concrete type is erased inside the box, so a caller can only print it, walk `source()`, or try `downcast_ref`. Have a look at `question_mark.rs` line 36 and tell me what that line would have to be if `err` were a plain `ParseIntError` instead of a `Box<dyn Error>`.","topic":"From into Box<dyn Error>"}}

